TI  - LLM Confidence Paradox
AB  - This research project empirically investigates the "Confidence Calibration Paradox," a core tension in designing trustworthy AI. The paradox arises because Large Language Models (LLMs) often express themselves with an assertive linguistic style that is misaligned with their internal certainty (a "confidence miscalibration"), yet human users are known to be more persuaded by confident communication. We question whether designing AIs to be epistemically honest (transparently showing uncertainty) might paradoxically reduce user trust, while AIs designed for social effectiveness (using confident language) risk propagating misinformation. To address this, we will conduct a pre-registered series of four iterative online experiments. These studies will causally estimate the effects of manipulating LLM linguistic assertiveness (e.g., hedged vs. assertive language) and numerical confidence disclosure (showing or hiding the AI's internal self-certainty score) on user Trust, Persuasion, and Behavioral Intentions. The experiments are designed to: Isolate the baseline effect of linguistic assertiveness alone. Determine the main causal effect of disclosing the AI's numerical confidence score. Test the interaction between linguistic assertiveness and numerical disclosure to see how users respond to (mis)aligned signals. Evaluate an alternative interface design (prompting users to estimate AI confidence) as a potential strategy for improving user calibration. Expected Outcomes: This research program will provide a causal map of how users respond to different AI confidence communication strategies. The findings will offer direct evidence on the nature of the Confidence Calibration Paradox and yield actionable design guidelines for building LLM interfaces that foster more appropriately calibrated user trust. We also expect to identify individual characteristics (e.g., numeracy) that may make users more or less susceptible to being influenced by miscalibrated AI confidence expressions.
