TY  - JOUR
TI  - Adaptive Trust-Aware Coordination Framework for Reliable LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems
AB  - With the advent of large language models (LLMs), multi-agent systems have become a promising framework for tackling complex tasks that involve decomposition, role specialization, discussion, verification, and consensus-building. However, reliability is a significant constraint due to the factual accuracy, reasoning quality, evidence support, and consistency of the participating agents, as well as the fact that many existing systems assume identical communication strategies for their outputs. In this paper, an Adaptive Trust-Aware Coordination Framework (TAC-MAS) for reliable LLM-based multi-agent systems is proposed. TAC-MAS models agent reliability as a dynamic state based on four complementary signals: historical task correctness, cross-agent consistency, evidence quality, and calibrated confidence. A temporal update mechanism enables evolution of trust between tasks, and a conflict detection mechanism raises potential conflicting claims and triggers specific re-verification only when it is required. Final decisions are made by consensus (not equal voting) and are based on trust. The evaluation is based on MultiAgentBench, and the measures include task score, coordination score, error rate, token cost, latency, and trust calibration. TAC-MAS is then contrasted with relying on single agent inference, majority voting, multi-agent debate, and static trust aggregation, and with ablation studies that reveal the effects of the evidence assessment, tempo-ral update, conflict detection, and adaptive weight. The framework offers a structured approach to research on reliability-aware coordination in LLM multi-agent systems and for minimizing error propagation without the need for retraining.
