TI  - Travelers’ Confidence in ChatGPT and their Willingness to Use it as a Source of Travel-Related Information
AB  - The study employs the stimulus–organism–response perspective to clarify how tourists develop intentions to use ChatGPT when accessing travel services. The research highlights how perceptions of intelligence, credibility, warmth, and communication speed act as stimuli that build tourists’ trust in ChatGPT and ultimately determine their intention to use it. A total of 409 valid survey responses were gathered from participating tourists. Analyses were carried out in SPSS Amos 24.0 and SPSS 23.0 using structural equation modeling. The predictors perceived intelligence, perceived credibility, perceived warmth, and communication speed significantly influenced tourists' trust in ChatGPT. Trust also strongly affected users’ intentions to rely on ChatGPT for travel services. Practical Implications – The study’s results highlight various practical takeaways. First, ensuring travelers’ trust in ChatGPT is essential for travel service companies. Second, travel providers can enhance tourists’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT by emphasizing features that signal the system’s intelligence, credibility, warmth, and communication speed. Finally, travel agencies should actively leverage these unique characteristics of ChatGPT—perceived intelligence, perceived credibility, perceived warmth, and rapid communication to boost customer engagement and service quality. Research on AI use in tourism and hospitality tends to prioritize technology acceptance, and far less attention has been directed toward understanding users’ trust and their behavioral intentions. To bridge this theoretical shortfall, the study explores the effects of perceived intelligence, credibility, warmth, and communication speed on tourists’ trust in ChatGPT. The findings align with the principles of the stimulus–organism–response theory. Keywords: AI, ChatGPT, perceived intelligence, perceived credibility, perceived warmth, communication speed, trust, behavioral intention
