TI  - Designing AI-based Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS) to Foster Clinicians' Appropriate Reliance: A Scoping Review
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI)-based clinical decision support systems (CDSS) are moving quickly into everyday diagnosis, treatment planning, and risk prediction, which raises a question central to patient safety: how do clinicians decide whether to trust what the AI tells them? Two failure modes bracket the ideal—over-reliance (following a recommendation without examining it, automation bias, and complacency) and under-reliance (dismissing a correct recommendation and algorithm aversion). What we want instead is appropriate reliance: trust calibrated to the system's actual accuracy and uncertainty, so clinicians go along with the AI when it is right and push back when it is wrong. A growing body of human–AI interaction research suggests this behavior can be designed for, but the evidence is scattered across ML/DL/LLM systems and across clinical, HCI, and other high-stakes fields and is reported in very different vocabulary. The primary aim of this scoping review is to identify and characterize the system design elements—spanning model system architecture, user interface, and user experience—that have been used to foster clinicians' appropriate reliance on AI-based CDSS. Following the JBI methodology and PRISMA-ScR, and using a Population–Concept–Context (PCC) framework, we will map these design elements across the literature. Secondly, we will chart the user-centered measures used to evaluate them (e.g., trust, reliance, override behavior, automation bias)—which also serve to scope what counts as "appropriate reliance"—and note how design elements have been paired with those measures. We will search PubMed, the ACM Digital Library, and Web of Science (2016–present), screen in duplicate, and chart data using a piloted form. Expected outcomes: a structured map and typology of the design elements used to support appropriate reliance; a secondary account of the measures used to evaluate them; and the groundwork for an evidence-based design framework and guidelines for AI-based CPOE/CDSS.
