TI  - Displayed confidence amplifies reliance on AI verdicts: Benefits when correct, costs when wrong
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) produce fluent text that is often only partially correct; therefore, users must decide what to accept. How certainty is displayed may shape these decisions, independent of whether the underlying claim is correct. In a fully within-subject experiment ( N = 149), participants judged 16 general knowledge statements in a fixed order before and after viewing a simulated assistant's true/false verdicts accompanied by a high- or low-confidence badge. Verdict accuracy was fixed at 50%, and accuracy and confidence displays were assigned orthogonally; therefore, the badge carried no information about the probability that a verdict was correct. The high-confidence display amplified verdict adherence, both when the verdict was accurate and when it was inaccurate. The amplification was double-edged; final accuracy changed by +4.2 percentage points (95% CI [0.4, 8.0]) when the verdict was correct and − 6.7 points (95% CI [−12.2, −1.2]) when it was wrong. Decomposing the two pathways showed that the badge display raised relative AI reliance and lowered relative self-reliance. Therefore, displayed confidence can influence decisions even without diagnostic value. These findings motivate testing whether calibrated claim-level confidence displays and evidence or verification pathways improve appropriate reliance.
