TI  - THE DOUBLE EDGE OF THE MACHINE: ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IN TRANSLATION AND ITS IMPLICATIONS FOR LANGUAGE EDUCATION
AB  - The rapid development of artificial intelligence (AI) – driven machine translation (MT) has changed how translation and language practitioners work with texts across languages. Machine translation tools such as Google Translate, DeepL, and large language model (LLM)-based systems currently offer instantaneous, fluent translations of source material, which is reshaping reading, writing, and cross-cultural communication practices. This article synthesizes recent research from TESOL, applied linguistics, and translation studies to examine advantages and disadvantages of AI-driven translation. It looks at accessibility, motivation, and learner confidence along with concerns about accuracy, overreliance, academic integrity, and the erosion of professional language development in language experts. The article closes with recommendations for applying AI translation tools in the translation practices in a way that supports, rather than replaces, human language translating.
