TI  - AI-Assisted Medical Diagnosis: A Hybrid XAI Framework for Interpretable Transformer-Based Text Classification
AB  - Text generation models show great promise in healthcare, but their 'black-box' nature hinders adoption because model output is not transparent enough to serve the needs of medical professionals. Although Explainable AI (XAI) techniques exist, model output remains hard to interpret and verify. This study proposes a novel approach to utilizing Large Language Models (LLMs) in a real-world medical use case: the classification of medical abstracts that doctors routinely consult to understand the condition of a patient combined with the generation of a text explanation of how the classification decision was made. The thesis develops and evaluates a hybrid XAI framework that combines classification and text generation that is designed to enhance the transparency, interpretability, and trustworthiness of AI use in patient condition classification scenarios. The hybdrid XAI framework is based on a BioBERT model, tuned on a Medical Abstracts dataset to classify diseases. It uses LIME (Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations) to extract the top contributing words that the model's classification decision is based on. And it features a structured prompt pipeline, designed through iterative prompt engineering, that integrates LIME output, prediction decision, as well as input text into a cohesive natural language explanation for medical professionals using GPT-4o (via Azure OpenAI Service). In sum, the framework produces explanations that state a patient condition prediction with confidence score, details the reasoning by linking LIME-identified words and communicates decision and reasoning to medical professionals for faster yet more transparent further decision-making. The system was evaluated using a survey among AI technology consultants that revealed that the system's explanations were highly interpretable and valued for increasing transparency. The survey results demonstrated a positive impact on user trust and revealed the framework's potential to detect model errors by highlighting contradictory evidence. In conclusion, this work demonstrates the potential of a hybrid LIME-LLM approach for generating more interpretable explanations for high-stakes classification scenarios such as patient condition decision-making. The study concludes with summarizing key limitations, such as its reliance on LIME's word-level analysis and limited evaluation without clinical experts. Building upon the foundation established by this thesis, future work is needed to evaluate model performance with medical professionals and explore the integration of more advanced XAI techniques. This study aspired to have contributed first steps to enhancing trust in AI for high-stakes applications.
