TI  - From assistants to auditors: opportunities and challenges of using general-purpose AI for auditing responsible AI systems
AB  - The rapid evolution of General-Purpose Artificial Intelligence Systems (GPAIS) has introduced new capabilities and significant governance challenges. As regulations impose stringent transparency, accountability and auditability requirements, evaluating responsible AI systems –namely, AI systems with built-in auditability and accountability mechanisms that support their lawful and ethical operation– becomes increasingly complex and resource-intensive. This paper examines GPAIS as enablers of scalable, evidence-based auditing of such systems, while recognising that their own risks and limitations constrain their suitability as auditors. Our primary contribution is GRAAF, a structured framework for integrating GPAIS into responsible AI evaluation pipelines, conceptualised as a multi-level fusion problem spanning data, models, agents, and audit judgements, and formalised through reliability-, disagreement- and uncertainty-aware fusion operators. GRAAF progresses from GPAIS as assistants to human auditors towards semi- and fully automated auditing agents. Through a taxonomy of use cases, operational metrics, and governance safeguards, we show how GPAIS can augment explanation generation, bias detection, robustness testing, and compliance monitoring. We also address risks of overreliance, adversarial manipulation, and audit capture, proposing mitigation strategies and a roadmap for safe adoption. We further instantiate GRAAF in a clinical triage drift-monitoring case study, in which a Large Language Model (LLM) auditor, meta-audited against deterministic reference rules, met all predefined success criteria while exhibiting limitations that preclude autonomous certification. Scalable AI auditing thus hinges on fusing heterogeneous evidence, multi-agent reasoning, and multimodal signals, with GPAIS as orchestrators; within layered oversight architectures, this fusion can make auditing more trustworthy and scalable without compromising human accountability.
