TI  - AI-Mediated Student Mental Health Support in Higher Education: An HCI-Oriented Study of Experiences, Risks, and Interactive AI Support Design
AB  - Student mental health has become a critical issue in higher education as academic pressure, financial stress, social adjustment, and uncertainty about the future continue to affect students’ emotional well-being. Although digital mental health therapies can enhance psychological well-being, their effectiveness often depends on context, participation, and design quality. At the same time, generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is becoming increasingly integrated into students' personal and academic lives, affecting their information seeking, stress management, emotional reflection, and reassurance-seeking behaviors. This study uses a Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) approach to examine how AI can enhance students' mental health in higher education. The paper weighs the advantages and disadvantages of adopting AI for mental health support by reviewing research on student well-being, digital mental health support, GenAI in education, AI-based mental health chatbots, and HCI design. While the analysis raises concerns about trust, privacy, misinformation, superficial empathy, overreliance, insufficient crisis escalation, and cultural mismatch, it also suggests that AI-based solutions may provide instantaneous, accessible, confidential, and stigma-free support. Based on these findings, the research proposes an HCI-informed framework linking student needs, AI interaction quality, psychological support mechanisms, risk protections, and well-being outcomes. According to the study, trust calibration, perceived empathy, disclosure comfort, autonomy support, emotional self-efficacy, ethical safety, and integration with human care should be considered when evaluating AI mental health support. The proposed framework integrates HCI interaction quality, psychological mechanisms, and risk-aware governance in one conceptual structure. The framework is explicitly positioned as a conceptual model that requires future empirical validation through expert validation, student testing, and prototype evaluation. This review provides a human-centered framework for creating trustworthy, responsible, and supportive AI systems that can improve students' mental health in higher education.
