TI  - USE OF GENERATIVE ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TOOLS AMONG HIGH SCHOOL STUDENTS
AB  - The study determined the commonly used Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) tools used by high school students and their extent of utilization to support their learning. It also determined the extent of manifestation of the positive and negative learning behaviors among the students in using Generative AI tools. Differences in the perceptions of the teacher-participants when grouped according to subject taught were also established. The study employed a descriptive-survey method using a survey questionnaire administered to 53 high school teachers, 50 Grade 9 students, and 52 Grade 10 students from the different schools in Buguias, Benguet. Frequency, weighted mean, and F-test were utilized for statistical analysis. Results revealed that ChatGPT, CICI, and Google Overview were the most commonly used GenAI tools by the students. GenAI was often used for comprehension support and task completion, and rarely for exploration, creativity, and assessment support. Positive learning behaviors were often manifested by students in using GenAI, particularly in terms of confidence, comprehension, and learning support. However, negative learning behaviors were also manifested, such as overreliance, using shortcuts to get answers, and less effort in doing school activities. No significant difference in the perceptions of teachers, according to subject taught, in terms of positive learning behaviors, but they differ significantly in terms of negative learning behaviors. Generative AI tools, particularly ChatGPT, provide significant support in enhancing students’ understanding and task completion, but their use also presents negative learning behaviors. Therefore, proper guidance and structured strategies from teachers are essential to maximize the benefits of Generative AI while minimizing its negative effects.
