TI  - How Students Adapt to Generative AI in Higher Education: Perceived Utility, Risk Salience, and Adaptive Learning Responses in China
AB  - As generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) becomes increasingly integrated into higher education, understanding how students adapt to AI-supported learning environments has become an important educational issue. While previous research has focused largely on technology acceptance and perceived usefulness, less attention has been given to how students simultaneously interpret the opportunities and risks associated with ChatGPT-like systems. This study examined associations among GenAI use, perceived utility, perceived risk salience, and adaptive learning responses among 947 university students in China. Using cross-sectional survey data, the analyses combined correlation analysis, hierarchical regression modelling, and exploratory cluster analysis. The findings showed that broader GenAI use was positively associated with both perceived utility and perceived risk salience. However, perceived risk salience demonstrated a stronger relationship with adaptive learning responses than perceived utility. Students who reported greater awareness of risks such as misinformation, overreliance, academic integrity concerns, and future skill uncertainty were more likely to engage in reflective and preparation-oriented learning behaviours. Cluster analysis further revealed substantial heterogeneity in patterns of student engagement with GenAI. The findings suggest that higher education responses to GenAI should support not only technical competence, but also critical judgement, reflective engagement, and adaptive learning practices.
