TI  - From Scaffolding to Substitution? Generative AI, Self-Regulation, and Learner Agency in EFL Academic Writing in Benin
AB  - Abstract Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is now routine in university writing, making simple adoption studies increasingly uninformative. The more consequential question is how GenAI enters the writing process, how learners regulate that support, and when assistance begins to substitute for learner effort. This explanatory sequential mixed-methods study examined AI-mediated academic writing among 186 undergraduate EFL students across three academic levels in Benin and integrated the quantitative results with thematic frequencies from 24 English lecturers. Student data captured tool use, AI support across pre-writing, drafting, revising, and proofreading, perceived linguistic, cognitive, and affective support, self-regulated writing behaviours, perceived writing benefit, and concerns about overreliance, verification, integrity, and critical engagement. ChatGPT was the most widely used platform (86.0%), but platform multiplicity itself was unrelated to perceived writing benefit (Spearman rho = .017, p = .814). By contrast, frequency of AI use was strongly associated with perceived benefit (rho = .615, p < .001), writing confidence (rho = .695, p < .001), and self-regulated writing (rho = .869, p < .001). AI support was reported most strongly during revising and proofreading (both M = 4.16 on a five-point scale), significantly above pre-writing and drafting. A notable benefit-risk coupling also emerged: students who reported greater benefit simultaneously reported stronger concerns (rho = .522, p < .001). Lecturer themes converged with this pattern, especially on linguistic scaffolding (87.5%), revision support (91.7%), overreliance (83.3%), and authorship or academic-integrity concerns (79.2%). The findings suggest that intensity and regulation of use matter more than the number of tools adopted. They support a view of GenAI as regulated scaffolding whose educational value depends on learners' capacity to plan, monitor, evaluate, and retain ownership of academic writing. Because the study relies on self-report and coded lecturer perceptions, it does not claim objective writing gains or causal effects.
