TY  - RPRT
TI  - How Answers Feel: The Role of AI Personalities and AI Disclaimers in Shaping User Reliance on and Persuasion by AI Assistants in Financial Decision-Making
AB  - OpenAI’s release of ChatGPT-5 included personalisation features that improve the communicative style of their conversational AI assistant (CAIA). These features enable users to personalise the communication style manually by adjusting the CAIA’s characteristics and selecting a predefined base style and tone categorised in seven different personalities. These personalities vary in their linguistic style and fine-tune the way ChatGPT responds (OpenAI, 2025a). OpenAI asserts that “the personality does not affect […] the rules it follows for safety” (OpenAI, 2025b). However, how these linguistically expressed personalities affect users is largely unexplored (Baines et al., 2024; Genç et al., 2026; Shi et al., 2020; Stöckli et al., 2024), especially with respect to the perception and usage of AI-generated recommendations (Al-Meqdadi, 2026; Kim et al., 2025). Current research about personalised linguistic styles in CAIA, such as Genç et al. (2026) and Shi et al. (2020), found that the linguistic style can influence users’ perceptions of and decision-making with CAIAs. CAIAs are employed in a variety of low and high-stakes environments to gratify utilitarian, hedonic and social needs (Lin & Ng, 2025). Particularly, the field of finance is a high-stakes domain that benefits from AI in gratifying utilitarian needs for work-related and personal demands, where individuals rely on AI-generated advice to support their financial decision-making. Depending on the linguistically expressed personality, the same AI advice can be perceived differently. Especially in text-based conversations, users rely on linguistic and stylistic features due to the unavailability of contextual and nonverbal cues (Sun & Wang, 2026). This is why human-human communication and sociolinguistic research emphasise the importance of choosing and expressing words wisely. An individual’s choice of words matters not solely for conveying the content of one’s message but also how the receiver will interpret the message and perceive the communicator. Therefore, personalisation based on human-like behaviour and AI responsiveness are cues that can increase attitudinal and behavioural trust (Keng et al., 2025; Kim et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2023), but also negatively impact users’ critical thinking ability (Kulal, 2025). AI advice can cause users to shift their confidence by making them feel insecure about their initial choice, as CAIAs are used for information seeking and feedback to find reassurance of their own thoughts. This shift in confidence is an indicator of persuasion and reliance (Schultze et al., 2015) alluding to users’ irrational tendency to seek and follow AI advice (Küper & Krämer, 2024). Drawing upon the Elaboration Likelihood Model (ELM), there are two routes of persuasion that explain how individuals get persuaded by messages. The central route relies on strong arguments, consciousness and logical thinking (Braca & Dondio, 2023), e.g. message content and perceived expertise. The peripheral route focuses on superficial cues derived from users’ instincts and emotions (Braca & Dondio, 2023), emotional intensity and human-likeness. One of the most efficient and used persuasion techniques is the modification of linguistic features (Blankership & Craig, 2011). These linguistic features influence the peripheral route through variation in language style, variability, intensity, and framing (Braca & Dondio, 2023). Hence, linguistically expressed AI personalities may serve as tools for persuasion (Genç et al., 2026) as the perceived identity of a CAIA can impact the persuasion outcomes and interpersonal perceptions (Sabour et al., 2025; Shi et al., 2020). Also, the perceived rational and relational attributes of CAIAs foster cognitive and affective trust (Al-Meqdadi, 2026; Keng et al., 2025; Lankton et al., 2015). Based on the Computers Are Social Actors and the Media Equation Theory, humans have a natural tendency to unconsciously apply social rules, norms and expectations to technologies. Although humans are fully aware of interacting with non-living entities (Baines et al., 2024; Krook, 2025). Hence, this effect of perceived human identity makes it challenging to distrust and avoid relying on CAIAs. User characteristics are key factors that impact the utilisation of advice (Küper & Krämer, 2024). Users can protect themselves from persuasive effects based on their need for cognition (NFC) and their expertise about AI (AI Literacy). Although NFC can be linked to the central route of persuasion (Blankenship & Craig, 2011), users’ willingness to process information that requires cognitive effort can help decipher elicited responses (Kim et al., 2025) as higher levels of NFC can reduce user reliance on CAIA (Goswami, 2025), unlike individuals with low NFC who avoid cognitive demanding tasks. Combined with AI literacy, users might be more aware of AI-related risks and errors, mitigating the impact of trust on reliance by preserving critical engagement with AI-generated information (Kulal, 2025). Thus, AI literacy has become an important enabler for informed decision-making (Schüller et al., 2023) by contributing to enhanced financial decision-making (Burdur & Gürsoy, 2025). Besides user characteristics, transparency design interventions might establish a safe and appropriate use of CAIAs that prioritise user safety by fulfilling the transparency requirements proposed by the EU AI Act. A way to abide by the EU AI Act in a proactive manner is to implement AI disclaimers that aim for careful and saturated decision-making. However, there is still uncertainty regarding the effectiveness of the proposed transparency requirements. Depending on the decision-making context, it is crucial to find out which kind of transparency is effective (De Fine Licht & De Fine Licht, 2020). This combination of linguistically expressed AI personality and transparency cues as AI disclaimers has not been investigated yet. Thus, it is unknown whether transparency cues might be counterproductive by creating a false sense of trust (Ho & Caals, 2024) and amplifying the persuasive effect of AI personality on users’ financial decision-making. AIM This proposed study aims to investigate how linguistically expressed personalities in CAIAs (default, friendly, professional) influence users’ reliance on AI advice in financial decision-making context by testing how varying transparency interventions affect this interplay, by focusing on: 1. how different linguistically expressed personalities impact decision-making (reliance, persuasion, and trust). 2. how user characteristics (NFC, AI Literacy) impact decision-making (reliance, persuasion, confidence shift and trust). 3. how transparency interventions (standard vs enhanced AI disclaimer) impact the effect of linguistically expressed personalities on decision-making.
