TI  - Revisiting Perceptual Distortions in the Age of Generative Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Perspective on “Alice in Wonderland Syndrome”
AB  - The phenomenon termed Alice in Wonderland syndrome (AIWS) was first described by Todd in 1955 as a neuropsychiatric disorder characterized by perceptual distortions in body image, time, and spatial relationships.[1] Although classically associated with migraine, temporal lobe epilepsy, and Epstein–Barr virus infection, the condition’s conceptual resonance extends far beyond neurology. In today’s context, particularly among Generation Z, the increasing cognitive and creative dependence on generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) presents a novel, technology-induced parallel to AIWS – where perception, proportion, and reality become algorithmically reframed. Clinically, AIWS involves transient dysfunction in visuospatial processing within the temporoparietal–occipital junction, resulting in micropsia, macropsia, or teleopsia.[2] Analogously, the pervasive use of generative systems – text-to-image models, artificial intelligence (AI)-driven design, and automated writing assistants – may induce a form of digital perceptual adaptation, subtly modifying how young users visually and cognitively interpret the world. The ophthalmologic and neurocognitive processes underlying visual realism are increasingly modulated by synthetic imagery, potentially recalibrating baseline perceptual expectations and esthetic standards.[3] From a psychiatric standpoint, prolonged reliance on generative systems may mirror derealization or depersonalization phenomena, where the sense of authenticity and self-generated creativity becomes diffused.[4] Generation Z, being digital natives, often experience identity formation within hybrid human–machine environments, where “authorship” itself is algorithmically co-constructed. Recent studies in digital psychiatry and cognitive neuroscience suggest that immersive engagement with AI-generated content can alter attentional control, dopaminergic reward pathways, and creative self-efficacy, leading to emotional flattening and reduced originality.[5,6] Thus, the parallel between AIWS and the widespread use of generative AI is not merely metaphorical but neurocognitively relevant. Both involve altered perceptual integration – whether through neurological dysfunction or technological mediation. As AI systems increasingly shape the sensory and cognitive experiences of younger population, interdisciplinary inquiry integrating neuro-ophthalmology, psychiatry, and digital cognition is imperative. Understanding how algorithmic environments distort perceptual and creative baselines may prove as critical to mental health as understanding visual distortions was to neurology in Todd’s time. A study demonstrated by Lee et al. in 2025[7] concluded that 319 knowledge workers who use GenAI technologies (such as ChatGPT and Copilot) at work at least once a week to see how they apply critical thinking and how GenAI influences their perception of critical thinking effort. After analyzing 936 examples of real-world GenAI tool use provided by the participants, they discovered that knowledge workers primarily employ critical thinking to ensure the quality of their work, such as by cross-referencing results with external sources. Furthermore, although though GenAI can increase worker productivity, it can also prevent critical engagement with work, which may result in a long-term overreliance on the technology and a decline in the ability to solve problems on one’s own. Less critical thinking effort is associated with greater confidence in GenAI’s task performance. When utilizing GenAI tools, critical thinking effort moves from information collection to information verification, from problem-solving to AI answer integration, and from task execution to task stewardship. The integration of GenAI into knowledge workflows presents new critical thinking problems for knowledge workers. Therefore, their research indicated that GenAI tools should be created to help knowledge workers think critically by addressing their awareness, motivation, and skill obstacles. Yet, this is not a call to reject AI. Rather, it is an invitation to reclaim agency in how we use it. If Gen Z is to shape the next era of knowledge and art, we must approach generative systems not as replacements for thought, but as instruments of reflection. True creativity demands awareness of what came before and the courage to push beyond algorithmic familiarity. Otherwise, like Alice, we risk being enchanted by a Wonderland where innovation looks infinite – but is, in truth, only a hall of mirrors reflecting history. Financial support and sponsorship Nil. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest.
