TY  - JOUR
TI  - AI and the double competence gap in dental education: towards symbiotic intelligence
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly becoming part of the knowledge infrastructure of healthcare. From diagnostic support and image interpretation to patient communication and clinical decision-making, AI-supported technologies, including generative AI, are changing the conditions under which healthcare professionals work. Dentistry is no exception. Recent research highlights emerging applications of AI in dental practice, while international reviews suggest growing opportunities and challenges associated with integrating AI into dental education. However, the rapid development of AI-supported technologies raises questions about how effectively professional education and continuing professional development can respond to evolving competency requirements. This raises a central question: How can dental education and continuing professional development respond to the potential double competence gap associated with AI-enabled dental practice?In this Opinion article, we address this question by proposing a conceptual framework that distinguishes between an initial education-practice gap and a continuing professional competence gap. Drawing on the concepts of AI literacy, Professional Digital Competence, and symbiotic intelligence, we discuss how dental education may develop and assess the competencies required for critical, ethical, and responsible AI use while preserving independent clinical reasoning, professional accountability, and patient-centred care.The rapid development of artificial intelligence raises important questions about how dental education can maintain the relevance of its curricula while safeguarding established standards of clinical competence, patient safety, and professional accountability. Although international reviews indicate growing interest in AI applications within dental education, systematic curricular integration appears to vary across institutions and educational contexts (9,10,24). Such variation should not necessarily be interpreted as resistance to technological change, as cautious implementation may also reflect legitimate concerns about educational quality, clinical validity, ethical safeguards, and the availability of robust evidence. Preliminary analyses of regulations and national curriculum frameworks for professional education raise questions about whether current educational provision adequately addresses emerging AIrelated competency requirements, potentially contributing to the two dimensions of the proposed double competence gap. Preliminary analyses of regulations and national curriculum frameworks for professional education suggest that this may contribute to reinforcing an already existing double competence gap When generative artificial intelligence became widely accessible through platform-based services such as ChatGPT in the autumn of 2022, many assumed that it would primarily affect the technology sector. However, when we tested a professional version of ChatGPT (GPT-4) in medicine, and partly in dental and psychology education, in March 2023, it was already apparent that this represented a technological paradigm shift with potentially farreaching implications across professional education programmes (1,2).While AI capabilities have developed rapidly in recent years in the health sciences (3)(4)(5)(6)(7)(8)(9)(10)(11), their integration into dental education presents a different set of challenges. Curriculum development, faculty preparation, assessment design, and clinical governance require careful consideration and cannot necessarily follow the pace of technological innovation. Recent reviews indicate that AI-related educational initiatives are emerging, but their integration into formal curricula and assessment practices remains variable. Several key explanations emerge from the literature: already overcrowded curricula, insufficient profession-specific digital competence among educators, and a lack of structured implementation (9,10,24). This potential misalignment between technological developments and structured educational provision provides an important rationale for examining the proposed double competence gap.Dental education has previously incorporated technologies such as digital radiography, simulation, intraoral scanning, and computer-aided design and manufacturing (29). These developments offer relevant lessons for AI integration: new technologies require faculty development, opportunities for supervised practice, alignment with clinical tasks, and assessment of what students can actually do. However, the current AI transition differs in its breadth and pace. Generative AI can be used across clinical reasoning, communication, learning, and assessment, including through tools that students access independently of their institutions. Moreover, the capabilities and limitations of these systems may change within a single curriculum cycle. Dental programmes should therefore draw on established approaches to curricular implementation while developing a more continuous process for reviewing AIrelated learning outcomes, teaching cases, and assessment practices.The Norwegian national curriculum regulations for healthcare professional education (RETHOS) emphasise that educational programmes should respond to society's current and future competence needs. However, AI is not explicitly addressed in the national curriculum regulations for dentistry, medicine, or psychology (12)(13)(14). Similarly, the Norwegian Agency for Quality Assurance in Education (NOKUT)'s national evaluation of medical education in 2025 provides limited explicit discussion of AI (15). These observations should be interpreted with caution. The absence of explicit AI terminology in national regulations or evaluation reports does not necessarily imply that AIrelated competencies are absent from institutional curricula, teaching practices, or assessment arrangements. National frameworks may deliberately emphasise enduring, technology-neutral professional competencies, allowing educational institutions to adapt their programmes to technological developments without requiring frequent regulatory revisions.Nevertheless, the limited explicit attention to AI in these national documents raises relevant questions about how emerging AI-related competencies are interpreted, operationalised, and assessed within individual educational programmes. International reviews have identified variation in the integration of AI into dental education, including challenges related to curriculum development, faculty preparedness, and the assessment of AI-related competencies (9,10,24).From the perspective of the proposed double competence gap framework, the central concern is therefore not simply whether AI is explicitly mentioned in national policy documents, but whether educational programmes systematically develop and assess the competencies required for responsible AI use in dental practice. Addressing this question requires empirical examination of institutional curricula, learning outcomes, educational activities, and assessment practices rather than relying solely on national policy analysis.Although the policy examples discussed here are drawn from Norway, the distinction between national competency frameworks, institutional curricula, and actual educational practices may also be relevant internationally. However, the extent to which the proposed double competence gap applies across different countries and dental education systems requires context-specific empirical investigation.The increasing integration of AI into healthcare knowledge infrastructures may also have epistemological implications for professional education. As AI systems become involved in information retrieval, diagnostic support, and clinical decision-making, questions arise concerning how professional knowledge is produced, evaluated, interpreted, and applied. These developments may gradually reshape aspects of professional roles and clinical reasoning, although their scope, pace, and educational consequences remain uncertain. Rather than predicting a specific timeline for such changes, dental education should develop adaptable approaches that enable students and practising professionals to critically evaluate emerging technologies while maintaining established standards of clinical expertise and professional accountability (16,18). Professional digital competence and AI literacy should become integral components of both the knowledge infrastructure and professional competence, and conceptual frameworks need to be developed that account for professionspecific AI use across both urban and rural patient contexts (17).At the same time, the challenge extends far beyond today's dentist education. The vast majority of today's practising professionals completed their education long before generative artificial intelligence became part of professional practice. Consequently, they have not necessarily received systematic training in how such AI systems can be used professionally, what their limitations are, or which ethical and legal challenges they may entail.We propose the concept of a double competence gap to describe two distinct but interconnected potential mismatches between the competencies required for responsible professional practice in increasingly AI-enabled dental healthcare and the competencies systematically developed through initial dental education and continuing professional development. A competence gap is understood here as a discrepancy between demonstrable professional capabilities and the knowledge, skills, and critical judgement required to perform relevant professional tasks safely, effectively, and ethically. Importantly, such gaps cannot be inferred solely from the availability of new technologies or the absence of explicit AI terminology in educational policy documents; their existence and extent require empirical investigation.The first competence gap, which we term the initial education-practice gap, concerns the potential misalignment between undergraduate dental education and the evolving competencies required in AI-enabled dental practice. International reviews suggest that the systematic integration of AI-related learning outcomes, teaching activities, and assessment practices in dental education remains uneven (9,10,24). This raises questions about whether existing educational provision adequately prepares students for the critical and clinically appropriate use of AI in future dental practice. Although students may independently engage with generative AI and other AI-supported technologies, such engagement does not necessarily constitute systematic development of profession-specific AI literacy. The central educational concern is therefore whether curricula, learning outcomes, teaching practices, and assessment arrangements adequately prepare students to critically evaluate AI-generated information, recognise its limitations, and integrate appropriate AI-supported decisionmaking with established clinical knowledge and professional judgement.The second competence gap, termed the continuing professional competence gap, concerns practising dentists whose initial professional education preceded the widespread availability of contemporary generative AI and other emerging AI applications. These professionals may encounter new technological expectations without having had equivalent opportunities for structured education in AI evaluation, clinical applicability, patient safety, data protection, and professional accountability. However, the extent of this gap is likely to vary across practitioners, clinical settings, previous digital experience, and access to continuing professional development.The conceptual contribution of the double competence gap framework lies in connecting these two educational challenges across the professional life course. Whereas AI literacy describes a set of relevant competencies, and Professional Digital Competence provides a broader perspective on technology-related professional capabilities, the double competence gap framework draws attention to the potential misalignment between competency requirements and educational provision at two different stages of professional development. It thereby offers a basis for examining how initial education, curriculum and assessment, faculty development, and continuing professional development can respond to changing professional requirements.We present the double competence gap as a conceptual and empirically testable proposition rather than as an established finding across dental education. Its educational significance depends on whether these proposed mismatches can be identified, assessed, and addressed in specific institutional and professional contexts.Empirically investigating the proposed double competence gap requires distinguishing between shortcomings in educational provision and demonstrable deficiencies in professional competence. The initial education -practice gap could be examined through curriculum mapping, IADT-guidelines (34IADT, 2026), analysis of intended learning outcomes and assessment practices, interviews with educators and students, and performance-based assessments of AI-related clinical competencies. The continuing professional competence gap could be investigated through research informed knowledge (33e.g. Ji et al., 2025), surveys, interviews, and scenario-based assessments involving practising dentists with different levels of professional experience and AI exposure. Importantly, the absence of explicit AI-related learning outcomes or structured training would indicate a potential gap in educational provision, but would not, in itself, demonstrate insufficient professional competence. Establishing a competence gap would require comparing observable performance with independently defined, clinically relevant competency standards, while accounting for differences in professional roles, practice contexts, and access to continuing professional development.Addressing the proposed double competence gap requires conceptual clarity regarding AI literacy, Professional Digital Competence (PDC), and symbiotic intelligence (SI). Although these concepts are closely related, they represent distinct dimensions of professional competence and human -AI interaction in dental education and practice.AI literacy refers to the knowledge, skills, and critical judgement required to understand, evaluate, and appropriately use AI systems within specific professional contexts. In dentistry, this includes the capabilities and limitations of AI, critically information clinical and relevant potential and patient information, and maintaining professional accountability. AI literacy therefore extends beyond to clinical reasoning, ethical and informed Digital Competence a of professional competence the critical, ethical, and clinically appropriate use of digital dentistry, includes established digital practices such as digital radiography, patient intraoral scanning, and clinical as as emerging AI applications. AI literacy can therefore be understood as a and increasingly important of rather than as an intelligence provides a conceptual perspective on how human professional expertise and AI capabilities may on previous on symbiotic intelligence in dental the potential for AI to rather than human clinical and professional In this AI may support information and the of diagnostic while dental professionals remain responsible for critical ethical patient communication, and clinical Importantly, symbiotic intelligence should be understood as a proposed to not as that AI-supported necessarily independent professional concepts have within the proposed double competence gap provides the broader professional competency AI literacy the AI-related capabilities required within that and offers a conceptual perspective on how these capabilities may be through critically informed human -AI The double competence gap framework, in attenti